{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent positive bias: model forecasts are generally too high versus realized prices, especially during downtrends. Apply a downward level shift (stronger in the first ~20 forecast days).",
    "Downtrend underestimation (lag): when the market sells off, the model follows the decline too slowly and stays above realized values for ~1–2 weeks. Increase sensitivity to recent negative momentum (steepen early-horizon slope).",
    "Trough overshoot: around local lows the model often projects a rebound that is too strong/too early; realized prices keep drifting lower. Damp mean-reversion signals immediately after sharp drops.",
    "Late-horizon catch-up: errors shrink and can flip sign near the end of the 30-day horizon (model sometimes ends slightly below realized during rebounds). Use horizon-dependent correction: larger negative adjustment early, smaller (or none) late.",
    "Volatility smoothing mismatch: model path is smoother than realized; it misses the depth of drawdowns. Allow larger downside moves than upside moves (asymmetric adjustment) following negative shocks."
  ]
}
